Artificial intelligence applications are commonly associated with large cloud services. Users send a request over the internet, powerful servers process it, and the result is returned. However, smaller AI models can now run directly on personal computers. A model running on a user's own computer is commonly called a local model. Tools such as Ollama and other local inference applications make it easier to download and run language models on laptops and desktop computers. One major advantage of local AI is privacy. If a developer uses a local model to summarize source code or internal documents, the data may remain on the user's machine instead of being sent to an external AI service. Local models can also operate without an internet connection. This is useful for developers, researchers, and users working in environments with limited connectivity. Another advantage is experimentation. Developers can try different models, prompts, context sizes, and configurations without paying for every API request. This makes local models particularly useful for learning. However, local models also have limitations. Large AI models require significant memory and processing power. A normal laptop may be able to run a small model comfortably but struggle with a much larger model. Model size also affects quality. Smaller models are usually faster and require less memory, but they may not perform as well as larger cloud models on complex reasoning or knowledge-intensive tasks. Hardware matters significantly. Systems with powerful GPUs or large amounts of unified memory can run bigger models more efficiently. CPU-only inference is possible but can be considerably slower. Local models also do not automatically have access to current internet information. A downloaded language model mainly contains knowledge learned during training. If an application needs current weather, recent news, live stock prices, or company information, developers must connect the model to external data sources. This is where techniques such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation, or RAG, become useful. An application can retrieve relevant information from documents or databases and provide that information to the model as context. A hybrid architecture can combine both local and cloud AI. Sensitive or simple tasks may run locally, while complex tasks can be sent to a more powerful cloud model. For software engineers learning AI, local models provide an excellent environment for experimentation. They make concepts such as prompts, tokens, context windows, inference, embeddings, and RAG easier to explore.